.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt FIREWALL.NFT 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm firewall.nft
.Nd the one packet filter Novi ships
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/novi/firewall.nft
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an ordinary
nft(8)
ruleset in nftables' own format \(em not a serialisation of a
settings panel.
It is loaded when
.Va network.firewall = on
in
.Xr system.conf 5 :
.Nm novi-state apply
runs
.Nm nft Fl f
on it, and
.Cm off
deletes the table.
.Pp
Nothing supervises it.
The kernel holds the ruleset, so there is no daemon, and boot
convergence reloads it like any other declared state.
.Pp
It is repo content rather than something generated, for the same
reason
.Pa /etc/passwd
is: it is policy, and policy should show up in a diff.
Edit it if you need something else.
.Ss What the shipped ruleset does
.Bl -tag -width "forward" -compact
.It Sy input
Policy
.Cm drop .
Accepts established and related flows, drops invalid ones, accepts
loopback, accepts ICMP and ICMPv6, accepts DHCP replies, and accepts
the declared holes.
.It Sy forward
Policy
.Cm drop .
Nothing here forwards.
If Novi ever routes for anything, that is a deliberate change to this
file, not a default.
.It Sy output
Absent, deliberately.
A desktop that cannot make connections is not usable, and a filter
constraining processes run by the user who can also edit the filter
is theatre.
.El
.Pp
The table is called
.Sy novi
on purpose: the observer asks the kernel whether a table by that name
exists, so this ruleset can be turned on and off without touching
\(em or claiming \(em anything else somebody has installed in another
table.
.Ss ICMP is accepted, not dropped
A host that black-holes ICMP breaks path-MTU discovery for everyone
talking to it and is a worse neighbour than one that answers a ping.
.Dq Stealth
is not a security property when the packets that reveal you are the
ones your own TCP needs.
.Ss DHCP needs its own rule
Replies arrive before conntrack has anything to match:
udhcpc(8)
sends from 0.0.0.0:68 to 255.255.255.255:67, and the reply is a
broadcast that is not
.Dq related
to anything.
On a machine that gets its address by DHCP \(em the default \(em
leaving those two lines out means the firewall comes up and the lease
never renews.
.Ss The holes are configuration, and live elsewhere
.Va open_tcp
and
.Va open_udp
are
.Sy sets ,
empty in this file.
.Va network.firewall.allow
in
.Xr system.conf 5
fills them, and
.Xr novi-state 1
is the only thing that writes them.
.Pp
A port list belongs in the same document as the hostname, where
.Nm novi-state diff
can see it and a generation can roll it back; the shape of the filter
belongs here.
Empty sets match nothing, so on a machine that has declared nothing
those two lines are the same as not being here.
.Pp
.Sy Enabling a daemon does not open a port .
That something listens and that the world may reach it are two
decisions, and a firewall that opens itself because a service started
is a service registry with a policy file attached.
.Nm novi-state apply
says so out loud when it starts a service whose port is closed \(em
which is not drift, because both keys are exactly as declared.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/novi/firewall.nft" -compact
.It Pa /etc/novi/firewall.nft
This file.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Turn the filter on and open ssh:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# novi-state set network.firewall on
# novi-state set network.firewall.allow "22/tcp"
# novi-state apply
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr novi-state 1 ,
.Xr system.conf 5
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0016, RFC 0022.
.Sh CAVEATS
Ports are written as
.Sy numbers ,
never names.
nft(8)
resolves a name through
.Pa /etc/services ,
so a rule written with one means something different on a machine
whose table differs.
That rule could not be written wrongly before this system shipped a
services table; it can now, and a test enforces it.
.Pp
.Nm nft Fl f
is additive, so the converger deletes the table before reloading.
Without that, an edit which
.Em removed
a rule would leave the removed rule in place.
